<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sound Toll</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1429–1857</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sound Dues</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">The Sound</a> Toll was a duty levied by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> on ships passing through the Øresund between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Baltic, introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> around 1429 and collected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> under the guns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a>. It was a major source of royal revenue until it was abolished by international treaty in 1857.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sound-toll.jpg" alt="Sound Toll" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boeing720 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_%C3%98resund_new_version.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
